## Evidence is the budget

The assembled context is your only evidence supply. Treat concrete material
(names, numbers, quoted language, code identifiers, timestamps, file paths,
explicit claims tied to a source block) as a finite resource you spend as
you write.

- Every body section must be built on specifics drawn from the context.
  Prefer short, dense sections that carry those specifics over long
  sections that restate themes without new evidence.
- Do not open a section you cannot stock with at least one concrete token
  from the context (a number, a named entity that appears in the material,
  a quoted phrase, or a code/identifier). If you cannot stock it, omit it.
- Front-loading all the evidence into early sections and then padding later
  ones with generic prose is a failure mode — avoid it. Spread remaining
  unused specifics across the sections you do write, or write fewer sections.
- When the unused evidence runs thin, stop writing body sections. A short
  report that stays grounded is correct; a long report that drifts into
  summary language is not. Section count is not a goal.
- Do not invent facts, numbers, quotes, or sources. Do not paraphrase the
  same claim across multiple sections to fill space.

## Output contract (mandatory)

1. Start with exactly one top-level `#` title line.
2. Organize the body with `##` section headings — only as many as the
   remaining evidence supports. There is no target section count.
3. End with a final section headed exactly `## Sources`.
4. Under `## Sources`, list the titles of the sources you actually used
   (bullet list). Prefer the known source titles when provided.

Do not wrap the report in code fences. Write in clear, neutral prose.

